<h1>Bill Quinlan</h1>
<p>Attorney, former general counsel to the governor. Candidate for mayor of Chicago, on the ballot February 23, 2027.</p>
<p>General counsel to Gov. Rod Blagojevich from 2005 to 2008, chair of the youth agency Lawrence Hall, and a former Lincoln Park High School council chair; 56, from the Northwest Side, son of Richard J. Daley&#39;s corporation counsel. Entered in September and filed on the last day.</p>
<p><a href="/directdemocracy/app/candidate/y9NHaWwD6sefeXgZfUQgaxEOHCX2">Full platform, open to comments</a></p>
<p><a href="https://www.quinlanforchicago.com/" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign website (quinlanforchicago.com)</a></p>
<h2>What sets them apart</h2>
<p>Only Quinlan: A written 10-year budget plan in place of year-to-year fixes.</p>
<p>With most of the field: The six planks, on safety, schools, growth, and affordability, state goals most candidates share.</p>
<h2>In their words</h2>
<ul>
<li>A written 10-year budget plan instead of a yearly choice between higher taxes and blind cuts. <q>We are on the brink of financial collapse that was created by politicians that were worried about getting reelected.</q> (<a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/attorney-bill-quinlan-announces-run-for-chicago-mayor/" rel="nofollow noopener">CBS Chicago, Sept. 15, 2026</a>)</li>
<li>Says what City Hall is missing is leadership that decides for people and not for special interests. <q>I&#39;m willing to make decisions that matter for the people, not special interests.</q> (<a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/attorney-bill-quinlan-announces-run-for-chicago-mayor/" rel="nofollow noopener">Announcement video, Sept. 15, 2026 (CBS Chicago)</a>)</li>
<li>Add vocational and technical classes in the public schools.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Campaign money</h2>
<ul>
<li>Committee: Quinlan for Chicago</li>
<li>Raised: $282K</li>
<li>Spent: $78K</li>
<li>Cash on hand: $204K</li>
<li>Since the committee formed Sept. 15, 2026, through Sept. 30. Sixteen days of fundraising, no loans. <a href="https://elections.il.gov/CampaignDisclosure/CommitteeDetail.aspx?id=41442" rel="nofollow noopener">Source</a></li>
</ul>
<p>Money figures are each committee&#39;s quarterly reports to the Illinois State Board of Elections: spent is total expenditures from January 1 to September 30, 2026 (or since the committee formed), cash is funds available on September 30.</p>
<h2>Support</h2>
<p>Not listed in the ChicagoSpeaks poll, Sept. 2026 (Mansueto Institute / NORC). 0.0 points of support per $1 million spent.</p>
<h2>On money</h2>
<p>Calls the city&#39;s finances a collapse created by politicians worried about reelection, and would replace the yearly choice between higher taxes and blind cuts with a ten-year budget plan.</p>
<ul>
<li><q>We are on the brink of financial collapse that was created by politicians that were worried about getting reelected.</q> (<a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/attorney-bill-quinlan-announces-run-for-chicago-mayor/" rel="nofollow noopener">CBS Chicago, Sept. 15, 2026</a>)</li>
</ul>
<h2>Pizza</h2>
<p>No position on record.</p>
<h2>Also on the record</h2>
<ul>
<li>Represented the fired CPS chief Pedro Martinez for free, and sued (unsuccessfully) to make three rivals choose one office to run for. (<a href="https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/attorney-bill-quinlan-announces-run-for-chicago-mayor/" rel="nofollow noopener">cbsnews.com</a>)</li>
</ul>
